WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.660
Input validation confirms that data has the expected type, shape, size, range, and allowed values before the application relies on it.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.920
Good validation is usually allowlist based and tied to the purpose of the field.

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:23.180
It does not replace authorization, parameterization, or encoding, but it reduces ambiguity and helps the code fail safely when a value is outside the intended contract.

4
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.780
Injection prevention depends on safe APIs, not string assembly.

5
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:35.320
Parameterized queries or prepared statements help prevent SQL injection because data is passed separately from query structure.

6
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:50.300
Safe ORM usage can help when the ORM is used correctly, but reviewers should still watch for raw query escape hatches, dynamic filters, string-built expressions, and places where untrusted values influence commands, templates, or interpreters.

7
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:58.020
Command execution, template and expression evaluation, and unsafe deserialization deserve careful review at a high level.

8
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:09.480
The concern is not to demonstrate payloads, but to identify code paths where untrusted data controls behavior in an interpreter, parser, shell, expression engine, or object construction mechanism.

9
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:17.260
Reviewers should ask whether the feature is necessary, whether inputs are constrained, and whether safer APIs exist.

10
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.100
Output safety is context specific.

11
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:29.160
HTML text, HTML attributes, URLs, CSS, JavaScript, JSON, and DOM operations require different handling rules.

12
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:37.740
Output encoding reduces the chance that data becomes executable content in the wrong context, while safe rendering practices reduce dangerous DOM behavior.

13
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:48.080
Validation, parameterization, safe APIs, and context-aware encoding solve different problems, so reviewers should look for the right control in the right place.
